[test-suite name=ux-web ring=2
  [doc [#
Conformance fixtures for cx-x/ux-web — the WEB RENDERER of the UX projection
(#787). One of TWO peers over cx-x/ux's semantic vocabulary, not the privileged
one. EXPERIMENTAL x/-TIER (D3).

Normative source: design/787/787-phase0-spec.md, owner-RULED 2026-08-17.

  SOLE ATTRIBUTE AUTHOR (P0-30/P0-31). The pinned subset is the whole htmx
  surface the emitter may produce; `hx-on` is excluded PERMANENTLY, which is
  what keeps strict CSP possible. Enforcement is two-sided — the name gate at
  authorship, and a re-scan of the serialized bytes.

  EVERY HTMX SPELLING IS DERIVED. Since W5 nothing in a semantic tree names an
  htmx attribute, an event name, or a CSS selector: `hx-target` comes from a
  semantic `affects=` path through the same frag-id that minted the target's
  own id, and `sse-connect`/`sse-swap` come from the render context's binding
  for a logical feed name. A hand-written selector could drift from what the
  emitter minted, and in W1 did.

  STRUCTURAL ESCAPING (P0-32). Fragments compose as CXDM trees and serialize
  once through the html pack, so there is no interpolation surface.

  TOKENS, NEVER INLINE STYLE (P0-33). A theme's token values are TENANT data
  reaching a stylesheet, which is an injection surface exactly like markup.

Every expected output was re-derived by RUNNING the program (cx <file>).
#]]

 [# The full emitter golden. Every htmx attribute present is on the pinned list,
    the action posts to the shipped intent wire, and the readable address rides
    beside the derived id so the golden stays human-diffable.

    `hx-target` is DERIVED from the semantic `affects=` path using the same
    frag-id that minted the region's own id — so it cannot drift. W1 carried that
    selector by hand and had to recompute it in the service. #]
 [case id=web-001-region-and-action-golden level=core
  [tags ux web render golden htmx intent P0-11 P0-18 P0-31]
  [title 'rendered-fragment golden — region, placed card, and an action whose target is derived']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $ctx {route: "/orders", feature: "orders", path: "/", bind: [ux:render-ctx [swap default=outerHTML]]}]
  [$web:render
    [ux:region feature=orders path="/open-orders"
      [ux:card id=summary
        [ux:heading level=2 "Open orders"]
        [ux:action verb=place-order label="Place order" affects="/open-orders"]]]
    $ctx]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'<section class="ux-region" id="cx-0c97f153f9ac3076" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path /open-orders]\n]\n"><section class="ux-card" id="cx-af0e55ddefd36714" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path /open-orders/summary]\n]\n"><h2 class="ux-heading">Open orders</h2><form class="ux-action-form" method="post" action="/intent/place-order" hx-post="/intent/place-order" hx-target="#cx-0c97f153f9ac3076" hx-swap="outerHTML"><button type="submit" class="ux-action">Place order</button></form></section></section>'
#]]
 ]

 [# The keying ruling made visible in rendered output: the keyed row carries an id
    and its readable address, the unkeyed row carries neither — the enclosing
    fragment is then the smallest addressable unit and updates repaint the region. #]
 [case id=web-002-keyed-rows-golden level=core
  [tags ux web render golden row-key P0-10 P0-16]
  [title 'rendered-fragment golden — keyed rows addressed, unkeyed rows not']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[$web:render
  [ux:table id=grid
    [ux:rows [ux:row key=r1 [ux:cell "a"]] [ux:row [ux:cell "b"]]]]
  {route: "/o", feature: "orders", path: "/"}]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'<table class="ux-table" id="cx-9488f4b5edb0421b" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /o]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path /grid]\n]\n"><tbody class="ux-rows"><tr class="ux-row" id="cx-0c605a4e78d087b1" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /o]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path \'/grid/row[r1]\']\n]\n"><td class="ux-cell">a</td></tr><tr class="ux-row"><td class="ux-cell">b</td></tr></tbody></table>'
#]]
 ]

 [# THE subset list, verbatim. Additions are individual rulings; `hx-on` is
    excluded PERMANENTLY because it is what would force 'unsafe-inline' into
    script-src. hx-boost stands for the "excluded until individually ruled in"
    set. #]
 [case id=web-003-the-pinned-htmx-subset level=core
  [tags ux web htmx subset normative P0-31]
  [title 'the normative attribute set, with hx-on permanently excluded']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
[list [?splice [$web:htmx-subset]] [$web:htmx-allowed "hx-post"] [$web:htmx-allowed "hx-on"] [$web:htmx-allowed "hx-boost"]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list 'hx-get' 'hx-post' 'hx-target' 'hx-swap' 'hx-swap-oob' 'hx-trigger' 'hx-include' 'hx-indicator' 'hx-ext' 'sse-connect' 'sse-swap' true false false]
#]]
 ]

 [# Enforcement is TWO-SIDED: the name gate makes an off-list attribute
    unreachable at authorship, and this re-scans the serialized bytes so a path
    that bypassed the constructor still goes red. RE2 has no lookaround, so the
    `=\"` sentinel is matched and the name taken from the capture group — an
    attribute NAME, never a value, can reach here. #]
 [case id=web-004-off-subset-attributes-are-refused level=core
  [tags ux web htmx subset P0-31]
  [title 'the output-side scan refuses any hx-*/sse-* attribute off the pinned list']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
[list [?splice [$web:off-subset-attrs "<div hx-post=\"/x\" hx-target=\"#y\"></div>"]]
      [?splice [$web:off-subset-attrs "<div hx-on=\"click: x\" hx-boost=\"true\"></div>"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-attr-off-subset attr=hx-on] [ux-refusal code=ux-attr-off-subset attr=hx-boost]]
#]]
 ]

 [# Fragments compose as CXDM trees and serialize ONCE through the html pack
    (WHATWG rules, double-quoted attributes). There is no interpolation surface
    to inject into. #]
 [case id=web-005-escaping-is-structural level=core
  [tags ux web escaping P0-32]
  [title 'text and attribute values escape by construction, because nothing is concatenated']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[$web:render
  [ux:card [ux:text "R & D <team> \"quoted\""] [ux:field label="A & B" "<script>alert(1)</script>"]]
  {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/"}]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'<section class="ux-card"><p class="ux-text">R &amp; D &lt;team&gt; "quoted"</p><div class="ux-field"><span class="ux-field-label">A &amp; B</span><span class="ux-field-value">&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;</span></div></section>'
#]]
 ]

 [# A <script src=… integrity=…> element is SANCTIONED — that is precisely how the
    vendored kernel is served and hash-pinned. What is refused is an INLINE
    script: a <script> with no src, or any content between its tags. Both are
    checked, because either one alone would let the other through. #]
 [case id=web-006-csp-invariants level=core
  [tags ux web csp P0-33]
  [title 'no inline style, no inline script, no event-handler attribute — and a hash-pinned script is sanctioned']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
[list [?splice [$web:csp-violations "<div style=\"x\"></div>"]]
      [?splice [$web:csp-violations "<script>alert(1)</script>"]]
      [?splice [$web:csp-violations "<div onclick=\"x\"></div>"]]
      [?splice [$web:csp-violations "<script src=\"/static/htmx.min.js\" integrity=\"sha384-a\"></script>"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list [ux-refusal code=ux-inline-style] [ux-refusal code=ux-inline-script] [ux-refusal code=ux-inline-script] [ux-refusal code=ux-event-handler]]
#]]
 ]

 [# Token values are TENANT data reaching a stylesheet, which is an injection
    surface exactly like markup. Fail-loud: never a sheet with a silently dropped
    token. #]
 [case id=web-007-token-theme-to-stylesheet level=core
  [tags ux web theme tokens P0-33]
  [title 'a token document compiles to a generated stylesheet; an unsafe value refuses the whole sheet']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $ok  [ux:theme [token name=bg value="#111"] [token name=fg value="white"]]]
  [= $bad [ux:theme [token name=bg value="red;} body{display:none"]]]
  ([$web:token-value-safe "#111"], [$web:token-value-safe "red;}"],
   [$web:theme-refusals $bad], [$web:theme-css $bad])]
#]]
  [out-text [#
(true, false, [ux-refusal code=ux-bad-token-value token=bg], [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-bad-token-value token=bg]])
#]]
 ]

 [# Sound only because each <script> also carries the matching integrity= (CSP3
    matches a hash-source against an external script's SRI digest). `hx-on`'s
    permanent exclusion is what lets script-src omit 'unsafe-inline' at all. #]
 [case id=web-008-strict-csp-header level=core
  [tags ux web csp P0-33]
  [title 'the header pins each vendored script by the SRI digest of the bytes actually served']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[$web:csp-header ([ux:asset name="htmx.min.js" href="/static/htmx.min.js" sri="sha384-abc"])]
#]]
  [out-text [#
"default-src 'none'; script-src 'self' 'sha384-abc'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
#]]
 ]

 [# The semantic tree says `live=orders` — a logical name. The endpoint and the
    event name come from the render context, which is why the same declaration
    reaches the terminal renderer intact. Both output scans come back clean. #]
 [case id=web-009-the-live-region-rides-the-render-context level=core
  [tags ux web liveness sse P0-44 R5]
  [; R-A1 migration: absence contributes nothing (2026-08-25) ]
  [title 'sse-connect and sse-swap come from the feed BINDING, never from the tree']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctx [ux:render-ctx
             [feed name=orders endpoint="/stream" event="orders-changed"]
             [fragment affects="/live/open/rows" endpoint="/orders/rows"]
             [swap default=outerHTML]]]
  [= $ctx {route: "/orders", feature: "orders", path: "/", bind: $rctx}]
  [= $r [$web:render [$ux:feed "orders" "/open" "orders" [ux:text "live"]] $ctx]]
  [list $r [$web:off-subset-attrs $r] [$web:csp-violations $r]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '<section class="ux-region" id="cx-2905217911f2a7e8" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path /open]\n]\n" hx-ext="sse" sse-connect="/stream"><p class="ux-text">live</p></section>']
#]]
 ]

 [# The container id is DERIVED from the operation's element path with the same
    frag-id that minted it during the first render, so a patch cannot target a
    selector that no longer exists. This is the ONLY place hx-swap-oob is
    authored anywhere in the tree. #]
 [case id=web-010-patch-operations-lower-to-out-of-band-swaps level=core
  [tags ux web liveness oob P0-44]
  [title 'insert, update, remove and repaint become the only hx-swap-oob values the emitter authors']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $ctx {route: "/orders", feature: "orders", path: "/open"}]
  [= $ops ([ux:op kind=insert container="/open/rows" [ux:row key=o-3 [ux:cell "Wu"]]],
           [ux:op kind=remove container="/open/rows" key=o-1],
           [ux:op kind=repaint container="/open/rows" [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row key=o-2 [ux:cell "Lin"]]]])]
  [$web:render-ops $ops $ctx]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'<template><tr class="ux-row" id="cx-a0842b4d116cd0bc" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path \'/open/rows/row[o-3]\']\n]\n" hx-swap-oob="beforeend:#cx-7024cc013e20e9e5"><td class="ux-cell">Wu</td></tr></template><template><tr class="ux-row" id="cx-78b7a1c1b189d286" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path \'/open/rows/row[o-1]\']\n]\n" hx-swap-oob="delete"></tr></template><template><tbody class="ux-rows" id="cx-7024cc013e20e9e5" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path /open/rows]\n]\n" hx-swap-oob="true"><tr class="ux-row" id="cx-e77435da8491f34f" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path \'/open/rows/row[o-2]\']\n]\n"><td class="ux-cell">Lin</td></tr></tbody></template>'
#]]
 ]

 [# A filter is a QUERY: it commits nothing, so it READS with hx-get and swaps the
    collection in place. The debounced trigger is a RENDERER decision and is
    spelled here, not in the tree — the terminal renderer binds the same
    declaration to `/`. A filter whose affected collection has no endpoint bound
    renders as a box that filters nothing, so it refuses instead, for the same
    reason an unbound feed does. #]
 [case id=web-011-the-filter-reads-it-does-not-post level=core
  [tags ux web filter W5]
  [title 'a filter lowers to hx-get against its fragment endpoint, and an unbound one refuses']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctx [ux:render-ctx
             [feed name=orders endpoint="/stream" event="orders-changed"]
             [fragment affects="/live/open/rows" endpoint="/orders/rows"]
             [swap default=outerHTML]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $ctx {route: "/orders", feature: "orders", path: "/", bind: $rctx}]
  [= $ok  [ux:region feature=orders path="/live"
            [ux:filter name=q label="Find" affects="/live/open/rows"]
            [ux:table id=open [ux:rows id=rows [ux:row key=r1 [ux:cell "a"]]]]]]
  [= $bad [ux:region feature=orders path="/live"
            [ux:filter name=q label="Find" affects="/live/nowhere"]]]
  ([$web:render $ok $ctx], [$web:render $bad $ctx])]
#]]
  [out-text [#
('<section class="ux-region" id="cx-b9aa0e4902fe6a43" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path /live]\n]\n"><div class="ux-control-field ux-filter"><label class="ux-label" for="ux-filter-q">Find</label><input class="ux-control" type="search" id="ux-filter-q" name="q" hx-get="/orders/rows" hx-target="#cx-cb25c893adec8e5e" hx-swap="outerHTML" hx-trigger="keyup changed delay:200ms, search"></div><table class="ux-table" id="cx-70c02252bf05598f" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path /live/open]\n]\n"><tbody class="ux-rows" id="cx-cb25c893adec8e5e" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path /live/open/rows]\n]\n"><tr class="ux-row" id="cx-a20fa70a7778ad68" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /orders]\n  [feature orders]\n  [path \'/live/open/rows/row[r1]\']\n]\n"><td class="ux-cell">a</td></tr></tbody></table></section>', [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-unbound-filter filter=q] [ux-refusal code=ux-dangling-affects element=ux:filter affects='/live/nowhere']])
#]]
 ]

 [# An HTML number input round-trips through a double and would silently corrupt a
    value the command declared exactly — the projection must never quietly narrow
    a declared type. RENDERER-PRIVATE: the terminal renderer maps the same kinds
    to its own editors and is free to disagree, because `kind=` states the CX
    TYPE, not a widget. #]
 [case id=web-012-declared-kinds-map-to-controls level=core
  [tags ux web form carrier P0-17]
  [title 'a decimal or money stays TEXT, because it rides a string carrier']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?for [in $k ("int", "float", "money", "decimal", "text", "bool", "date", "instant")]
  [yield [$web:input-kind-of $k]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'number'
'number'
'text'
'text'
'text'
'checkbox'
'date'
'datetime-local'
#]]
 ]

 [# It reads the tree this renderer actually serializes, not a re-parse of the
    bytes — the tree IS the rendering, and re-parsing would test the html pack's
    parser instead of this lowering. Two recoveries are deliberate rather than
    cheats: a row's KEY comes out of data-cx-frag, which P0-11 says exists so a
    client can recover the address without a second registry, and a verb comes
    out of /intent/<verb>, which IS the command address. #]
 [case id=web-013-content-readback-from-the-html-tree level=core
  [tags ux web equivalence normal-form R5]
  [title 'the web side of the equivalence assertion, read back from the tree it serializes']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $t [ux:card [ux:heading level=2 "Open"] [ux:field label="Customer" "Ada"]
                 [ux:badge tone=warn "late"] [ux:action verb=place-order label="Go"]]]
  [= $ctx {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/"}]
  [list [= [$ux:content-repr $t] [$web:content-repr-html $t $ctx]] [$web:content-repr-html $t $ctx]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list true "[c:card\n  [c:heading level='2' Open]\n  [c:field label=Customer Ada]\n  [c:badge tone=warn late]\n  [c:action verb=place-order label=Go]\n]\n"]
#]]
 ]

 [# Never a partial render: the pipeline runs the SHARED semantic gate, the binding
    gates and the address check before lowering, and a refusal returns one [err]
    naming every violation. #]
 [case id=web-014-a-refused-tree-yields-no-markup level=core
  [tags ux web refusal]
  [title 'a projection failure never reaches a browser as content']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
([$web:render [ux:action verb=v target="#x"] {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/"}],
 [$web:render [ux:region feature=f path="/r" [ux:card id=d] [ux:card id=d]] {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/"}])
#]]
  [out-text [#
([err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-renderer-private-attr element=ux:action attr=target]], [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-address-reused id=cx-fa5c41a015045bf8]])
#]]
 ]

 [# A red border is not a message and a screen reader reads neither a colour nor
    a border, so `aria-invalid` is emitted next to the sentence rather than
    instead of it. #]
 [case id=web-015-a-refusal-lands-on-its-own-control level=core
  [tags ux web validation aria W7]
  [title 'the field is marked bad, the message sits with it, and a screen reader gets both']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $t [ux:form verb=place-order
          [ux:input name=customer label="Customer" error="who is this order for?"]
          [ux:submit label="Place order"]]]
  [$web:render $t {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/"}]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'<form class="ux-form ux-busy" method="post" action="/intent/place-order" id="cx-75350d510997b9f4" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /o]\n  [feature f]\n  [path /place-order]\n]\n" hx-post="/intent/place-order" hx-indicator="#cx-75350d510997b9f4"><div class="ux-control-field"><label class="ux-label" for="cx-75350d510997b9f4-customer">Customer</label><input class="ux-control ux-control-bad" id="cx-75350d510997b9f4-customer" name="customer" type="text" aria-invalid="true"><span class="ux-field-error">who is this order for?</span></div><button type="submit" class="ux-action ux-submit">Place order</button></form>'
#]]
 ]

 [# Removing an unsubmitted line is not an INTENT: nothing is committed and
    nothing is journaled, so it cannot ride the intent wire — the same
    reasoning that made `ux:filter` a query control. htmx replaces the
    instance with the response body, so an empty body IS the removal. The
    pinned subset already carries every attribute it needs. #]
 [case id=web-016-removing-a-line-is-an-empty-response level=core
  [tags ux web group remove hypermedia W7]
  [title 'the remove control is renderer-minted from the group’s declaration and needs no script']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctx [ux:render-ctx
             [feed name=orders endpoint="/stream" event="orders-changed"]
             [fragment affects="/live/open/rows" endpoint="/orders/rows"]
             [swap default=outerHTML]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $t [ux:group name=lines label="Lines" affects="/f/entry/place-order/lines" add-label="Add a line"
          [ux:card [ux:input name="lines.0.qty" label="Qty"]]]]
  [$web:render $t {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/entry/place-order", fragment: true, bind: $rctx}]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-dangling-affects element=ux:group affects='/f/entry/place-order/lines']]
#]]
 ]

 [# The strict rule — every affects= must name something the same tree mints —
    is right for a PAGE and wrong for a fragment: the form re-rendered after a
    refusal targets the card it is about to be swapped into, and that card is
    on the page, not in the payload. The relaxation is not "skip the gate": a
    fragment must still name a path the render context declares, so an
    affects= naming nothing anywhere still refuses. #]
 [case id=web-017-a-fragment-may-aim-at-the-page-it-is-swapped-into level=core
  [tags ux web refusal fragment affects W7]
  [title 'a fragment’s affects= is checked against the surface’s declaration, not against the payload']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctx [ux:render-ctx
             [feed name=orders endpoint="/stream" event="orders-changed"]
             [fragment affects="/live/open/rows" endpoint="/orders/rows"]
             [swap default=outerHTML]]]
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $t [ux:form verb=place-order affects="/f/entry" [ux:submit label="Go"]]]
  [= $bad [ux:form verb=place-order affects="/nowhere" [ux:submit label="Go"]]]
  ([$web:render $t   {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/", bind: $rctx}],
   [$web:render $t   {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/", fragment: true, bind: $rctx}],
   [$web:render $bad {route: "/o", feature: "f", path: "/", fragment: true, bind: $rctx}])]
#]]
  [out-text [#
([err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-dangling-affects element=ux:form affects='/f/entry']], [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-dangling-affects element=ux:form affects='/f/entry']], [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-dangling-affects element=ux:form affects='/nowhere']])
#]]
 ]

 [# P0-104: shared tokens outside any palette are written once; the default
    palette joins them in :root; each non-default palette re-states the same
    custom properties under its scheme's media condition. The refusals are
    fail-loud like every token's: a scheme outside the ruled set (or claimed
    twice) is ux-bad-palette-scheme; palettes without exactly one default=true
    are ux-bad-palette-default. A theme with no palettes emits byte-identically
    to the single-block form this generalizes. #]
 [case id=web-018-a-palette-is-a-scheme-conditioned-token-set level=core
  [tags ux web theme palettes P0-104]
  [title 'the default palette is :root, the dark palette rides prefers-color-scheme, and a malformed palette refuses the sheet']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [; R-A1 migration: envelope → spliced children (2026-08-25) ]
  [= $two [ux:theme [token name=gap value="4px"]
            [palette scheme=light default=true [token name=bg value="#fff"] [token name=fg value="#111"]]
            [palette scheme=dark [token name=bg value="#111"] [token name=fg value="#eee"]]]]
  [= $plain [ux:theme [token name=bg value="#111"] [token name=fg value="white"]]]
  [= $odd [ux:theme [palette scheme=sepia default=true [token name=bg value="#111"]]]]
  [= $twodefault [ux:theme [palette scheme=light default=true [token name=bg value="#fff"]]
                           [palette scheme=dark default=true [token name=bg value="#111"]]]]
  ([$web:theme-vars $two], [$web:theme-vars $plain],
   [$web:theme-refusals $odd], [$web:theme-refusals $twodefault],
   [$web:theme-css $odd])]
#]]
  [out-text [#
(':root{--cx-gap:4px;--cx-bg:#fff;--cx-fg:#111;}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){:root{--cx-bg:#111;--cx-fg:#eee;}}\n', ':root{--cx-bg:#111;--cx-fg:white;}\n', [ux-refusal code=ux-bad-palette-scheme scheme=sepia], [ux-refusal code=ux-bad-palette-default], [err code=ux-refused [ux-refusal code=ux-bad-palette-scheme scheme=sepia]])
#]]
 ]

 [# W23, audit2 letter (i): the product page says "two of them" where the
    decision happens. No wire of its own — the browser is the shell that steps
    it. The direct-entry input inside a verb-carrying quantity's pill posts the
    whole intent on enter, kernel-free, and the readback recovers the value
    from the input's value= (the text-content read died with the span). #]
 [case id=web-019-a-field-mode-quantity-is-the-forms-own-field level=core
  [tags ux web quantity field-mode readback P0-102 W23]
  [title 'verb-less quantity lowers to a labeled bounded number input; inside an action it rides that act’s post; both read back as c:quantity with verb stated empty']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $ctx {route: "/p/X", feature: "product", path: "/"}]
  [= $fld [ux:quantity name=qty label=Quantity value=2 min=1 max=9]]
  [= $act [ux:action verb=add-to-basket label="Add to basket"
            [ux:param name=sku value=X]
            [ux:quantity name=qty label=Quantity value=1 min=1 max=5]]]
  [list [$web:render $fld $ctx]
        [$web:content-repr-html $fld $ctx]
        [$web:content-repr-html $act $ctx]
        [$web:content-repr-html [ux:quantity name=qty verb=set-quantity value=3 min=1 max=9
                                  [ux:param name=sku value=X]] $ctx]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '<div class="ux-quantity ux-quantity-field" role="group" aria-label="Quantity" data-cx-name="qty" data-cx-verb="" data-cx-min="1" data-cx-max="9"><label class="ux-label" for="ux-qty-qty">Quantity</label><input type="number" class="ux-control ux-quantity-direct" id="ux-qty-qty" name="qty" value="2" min="1" max="9" step="1"></div>' "[c:quantity name=qty label=Quantity verb='' value='2' min='1' max='9']\n" "[c:action verb=add-to-basket label='Add to basket'\n  [c:param name=sku value=X]\n  [c:quantity name=qty label=Quantity verb='' value='1' min='1' max='5']\n]\n" "[c:quantity name=qty label=Qty verb=set-quantity value='3' min='1' max='9'\n  [c:param name=sku value=X]\n]\n"]
#]]
 ]

 [# W24: the region owns the ONE connection (web-009); these are the listeners
    under it. The nav item's push payload is its new TEXT — route-neutral, the
    href stays the receiving page's own. The aside cannot take pushed content
    at all: a fragment address is (route, feature instance, element path), so
    a pushed body would carry the PUBLISHER's targets — so it hx-gets the
    binding's refetch= endpoint with its own location as return=, triggered by
    sse:<event>, swapping itself whole. #]
 [case id=web-020-live-listeners-under-one-connection level=core
  [tags ux web liveness sse P0-44 W24]
  [title 'a live nav item listens with sse-swap on its anchor; a live OPEN aside refetches itself on the same event; a closed aside carries neither']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctx [ux:render-ctx [feed name=basket endpoint="/stream" event="basket-changed" refetch="/basket/aside"]
                          [swap default=outerHTML]]]
  [= $ctx {route: "/c/k", feature: "shell", path: "/shell", bind: $rctx}]
  [list [$web:render [ux:nav-item id=basket live=basket href="/c/k?basket=open" label="Basket · 1 item · $9.00"] $ctx]
        [$web:render [ux:aside label="Your basket" open=true live=basket dismiss-href="/c/k" [ux:text "a line"]] $ctx]
        [$web:render [ux:aside label="Your basket" open=false live=basket dismiss-href="/c/k" [ux:text "a line"]] $ctx]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '<li class="ux-nav-item" id="cx-563528cf560b374a" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /c/k]\n  [feature shell]\n  [path /shell/basket]\n]\n"><a href="/c/k?basket=open" sse-swap="basket-changed">Basket · 1 item · $9.00</a></li>' '<aside class="ux-aside ux-aside-open" aria-label="Your basket" hx-get="/basket/aside?return=%2Fc%2Fk" hx-trigger="sse:basket-changed" hx-target="this" hx-swap="outerHTML"><div class="ux-aside-head"><h2 class="ux-aside-title">Your basket</h2><a class="ux-aside-dismiss" href="/c/k" aria-label="Close Your basket">×</a></div><div class="ux-aside-body"><p class="ux-text">a line</p></div></aside>' '<aside class="ux-aside" aria-label="Your basket"><div class="ux-aside-head"><h2 class="ux-aside-title">Your basket</h2></div><div class="ux-aside-body"><p class="ux-text">a line</p></div></aside>']
#]]
 ]

 [# ── EDIT MODE (P0-109/P0-110, W26 — the studio) ─────────────────────────────
    The studio is this emitter with edit engaged: same projection, same
    lowering, plus the selection stamps. Edit data rides the RENDER CONTEXT
    ([edit], [sel-placed], [sel-prov], [sel-claim] children), so the fragment
    context map never changes shape and a normal render is byte-identical by
    construction — web-022 pins the absence, these pin the stamped bytes. #]

 [# a placed element (author-assigned id) resolves to the regime-4 target
    `[placed <id>]`; the inspector's facts — hint provenance by cascade level,
    the component, container-ness — ride beside it, emitter-authored. #]
 [case id=web-021-edit-mode-stamps-the-placed-target level=core
  [tags ux web edit studio sel P0-109 W26]
  [title 'edit mode stamps data-cx-sel [placed id] with provenance and component facts']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctxE [ux:render-ctx [swap default=outerHTML]
              [edit components="hero promo group" return="/?edit=1"]
              [sel-placed id=hero component=hero container=false]
              [sel-prov id=hero hint=tone level=surface]]]
  [= $cE {route: "/", feature: "home", path: "/", bind: $rctxE}]
  [$web:render [ux:card id=hero [ux:heading level=2 "Hello"]] $cE]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'<section class="ux-card" id="cx-d4671953f6aa8281" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /]\n  [feature home]\n  [path /hero]\n]\n" data-cx-sel="[placed hero]" data-cx-sel-prov="tone:surface:" data-cx-sel-component="hero" data-cx-sel-container="false"><h2 class="ux-heading">Hello</h2></section>'
#]]
 ]

 [# SELECTION IS MEMBERSHIP (P0-123's sibling rule): an element carrying an id
    is a regime-4 target only when the arrangement actually holds a placed
    element of that id. A projection's OWN output — a department card, a
    product tile — has an id for addressing and fragment identity but is not
    something a layout command can name, so it must not be stamped selectable.
    Stamping it offered the studio a drag whose only possible answer was
    `ux-layout-address-miss`. #]
 [case id=web-021b-edit-mode-stamps-only-arrangement-members level=core
  [tags ux web edit studio sel P0-109 P0-123 W27]
  [title 'edit mode stamps a placed target only for an id the arrangement holds']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctxE [ux:render-ctx [swap default=outerHTML]
              [edit components="hero" return="/?edit=1"]
              [sel-placed id=hero component=hero container=false]]]
  [= $cE {route: "/", feature: "home", path: "/", bind: $rctxE}]
  [= $member [$web:render [ux:card id=hero [ux:text "in the arrangement"]] $cE]]
  [= $other  [$web:render [ux:card id=dept-kitchen [ux:text "a projection's own output"]] $cE]]
  [list [$contains [$string $member] "data-cx-sel=\"[placed hero]\""]
        [$contains [$string $other] "data-cx-sel"]
        [$contains [$string $other] "id="]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list true false true]
#]]
 ]

 [# a formatted field resolves to its regime-1 claim key — the resolution the
    projection computed anyway, stamped rather than re-derived (one resolver);
    and the SAME tree under a normal context carries NO data-cx-sel at all. #]
 [case id=web-022-edit-mode-claim-stamp-and-normal-mode-absence level=core
  [tags ux web edit studio sel claim P0-109 W26]
  [title 'a formatted field stamps its claim target; normal mode stamps nothing']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctxE [ux:render-ctx [swap default=outerHTML]
              [edit components="hero" return="/?edit=1"]
              [sel-claim label=Price addr=sha2-256:abc path="/price" prov=platform]]]
  [= $rctx0 [ux:render-ctx [swap default=outerHTML]]]
  [= $tree [ux:field label=Price format=money currency=USD "12.00"]]
  [= $hE [$web:render $tree {route: "/", feature: "home", path: "/", bind: $rctxE}]]
  [= $h0 [$web:render $tree {route: "/", feature: "home", path: "/", bind: $rctx0}]]
  [list $hE [$contains [$string $h0] "data-cx-sel"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '<div class="ux-field" data-cx-sel="[claim sha2-256:abc /price]" data-cx-sel-prov="platform"><span class="ux-field-label">Price</span><span class="ux-field-value" data-cx-format="money">$12.00</span></div>' false]
#]]
 ]

 [# a region whose feed binding carries region-refetch= LISTENS on the page's
    one connection instead of opening a second one (P0-44): hx-get itself on
    sse:<event>, rendered for its own location — while the binding without it
    keeps owning the connection (sse-connect). #]
 [case id=web-023-region-listens-and-refetches level=core
  [tags ux web live region refetch P0-44 W26]
  [title 'a region-refetch feed binding makes the region a listener, not a second connection']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctx [ux:render-ctx
             [feed name=layout endpoint="/stream" event="layout-changed" region-refetch="/home/body"]
             [feed name=basket endpoint="/stream" event="basket-changed"]
             [swap default=outerHTML]]]
  [= $c0 {route: "/", feature: "home", path: "/", bind: $rctx}]
  [= $listener [$web:render [ux:region feature=home path="/home" live=layout [ux:text "x"]] $c0]]
  [= $owner    [$web:render [ux:region feature=shell path="/shell" live=basket [ux:text "x"]] $c0]]
  [list $listener [$contains [$string $owner] "sse-connect"] [$contains [$string $listener] "sse-connect"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '<section class="ux-region" id="cx-61b6533d57b9b944" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /]\n  [feature home]\n  [path /home]\n]\n" hx-get="/home/body?return=%2F" hx-trigger="sse:layout-changed" hx-target="this" hx-swap="outerHTML"><p class="ux-text">x</p></section>' true false]
#]]
 ]

 [# ── the ARRANGED grid (W26 owner review): spans as surface data ─────────────
    An arrangement element's `span` hint realizes on THIS face as data-cx-span
    (and the arranged region as data-cx-arranged), driven by render-context
    entries — the semantic tree stays layout-blind (P0-20), the terminal face
    never reads them, and a context without the entries stamps nothing. #]
 [case id=web-024-arranged-region-and-spans level=core
  [tags ux web arranged span studio W26]
  [title 'an arranged region grids and a place-span stamps; a plain context stamps neither']
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-x/ux' :as ux]
[?lib 'cx-x/ux-web' :as web]
[?let
  [= $rctx [ux:render-ctx [swap default=outerHTML]
             [arranged feature=home]
             [place-span id=featured span=6]]]
  [= $c0 {route: "/", feature: "shell", path: "/", bind: $rctx}]
  [= $tree [ux:region feature=home path="/home"
             [ux:card id=hero [ux:text "h"]]
             [ux:card id=featured [ux:text "f"]]]]
  [= $h [$web:render $tree $c0]]
  [= $plain [$web:render $tree {route: "/", feature: "shell", path: "/", bind: [ux:render-ctx [swap default=outerHTML]]}]]
  [list $h [$contains [$string $plain] "data-cx-arranged"] [$contains [$string $plain] "data-cx-span"]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[list '<section class="ux-region" id="cx-61b6533d57b9b944" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /]\n  [feature home]\n  [path /home]\n]\n" data-cx-arranged="1"><section class="ux-card" id="cx-6d329c0db3f3b12b" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /]\n  [feature home]\n  [path /home/hero]\n]\n"><p class="ux-text">h</p></section><section class="ux-card" id="cx-84144876512931d3" data-cx-frag="[frag\n  [route /]\n  [feature home]\n  [path /home/featured]\n]\n" data-cx-span="6"><p class="ux-text">f</p></section></section>' false false]
#]]
 ]
]
